<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">East and Southern Africa</h1><p class="path-tagline">Highland Christian kings, Swahili merchant towns, plateau gold and the kingdoms that faced Portugal and Britain.</p><p class="path-intro">Aksum, in highland Ethiopia and Eritrea, grew rich on the Red Sea trade and under King Ezana became one of the first states to adopt Christianity. On the coast, Swahili merchant towns traded across the Indian Ocean from around the 8th century, and island Kilwa controlled the gold trade from Sofala. That gold came from the interior, where Mapungubwe, Great Zimbabwe and the Mutapa Empire rose one after another. The last stops turn to kingdoms that fought European expansion: Kongo and Ndongo against Portugal, and the Zulu Kingdom, which routed a British force at Isandlwana in 1879.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Navigator</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a> · <a href="/start">the Strategist</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/aksumite-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ezana.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 100 CE – 940 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Aksumite Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Aksum grew rich trading ivory and gold through its Red Sea port of Adulis, and under King Ezana became one of the first states to adopt Christianity.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Aksumite Empire was a trading state centred on Aksum in the northern Ethiopian highlands and present-day Eritrea, flourishing from roughly the first to the seventh century CE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Work</span><span class="path-step-name">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela</span><span class="path-step-why">Medieval churches carved out of the rock in the northern Ethiopian highlands, linked with King Lalibela of the Zagwe dynasty and still a major site of pilgrimage.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The rock-hewn churches of Lalibela are a group of medieval churches carved directly out of rock in the town of Lalibela in the highlands of northern Ethiopia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/swahili"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · c. 800 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Swahili</span><span class="path-step-why">The Bantu speech of the East African coast took shape in merchant towns that traded across the Indian Ocean from around the 8th century, absorbing many Arabic words.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Swahili are a people of the East African coast, whose culture developed through interaction between Bantu-speaking Africans and Arab and Persian traders.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Place</span><span class="path-step-name">Kilwa Kisiwani</span><span class="path-step-why">Seat of the Kilwa Sultanate, which controlled the gold trade from Sofala; Ibn Battuta praised it in 1331, and the Portuguese sacked it in 1505.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Kilwa Kisiwani is an island off the southern coast of Tanzania, seat of the Kilwa Sultanate, which from the 13th century controlled the gold trade from Sofala.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mapungubwe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mapungubwe-hill.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 1220 CE – 1290 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kingdom of Mapungubwe</span><span class="path-step-why">Around 1220 its rulers moved onto a hill above the Limpopo, apart from the commoners below, and were buried with gold, among it the famous gold-foil rhinoceros.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kingdom-of-zimbabwe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 1220 CE – 1450 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kingdom of Zimbabwe</span><span class="path-step-why">A Shona kingdom rich on cattle and on gold and ivory sent to Sofala, whose dry-stone walls are the largest precolonial stone structures in southern Africa.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Kingdom of Zimbabwe was the state centred on Great Zimbabwe on the Zimbabwe plateau, which flourished from about the twelfth to the fifteenth century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/mutapa-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zambezi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1430 CE – 1760 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mutapa Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded around 1430 as Great Zimbabwe declined, this Shona state between the Zambezi and Limpopo traded its gold through Swahili and later Portuguese merchants.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Mutapa Empire, also known as the Mwene Mutapa state, was a Shona kingdom that emerged in the mid-fifteenth century between the Zambezi and Limpopo rivers, in present-day Zimbabwe and Mozambique.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kongo-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lukeni-lua-nimi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1390 CE – 1914 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kongo Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Ruled from Mbanza Kongo, it adopted Christianity and Portuguese literacy, and in 1526 Afonso I wrote to Portugal protesting the abuses of the slave trade.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Kingdom of Kongo was a Central African state centred on Mbanza Kongo in present-day northern Angola, flourishing from the fourteenth century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/njinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/njinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1583–1663</span><span class="path-step-name">Njinga of Ndongo and Matamba</span><span class="path-step-why">Queen of the Mbundu kingdoms of Ndongo and Matamba, she allied with the Dutch in the 1640s and resisted Portuguese expansion for decades, making peace in 1656.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Njinga Mbande was queen of the Mbundu kingdoms of Ndongo and Matamba in what is now Angola.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/zulu-kingdom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dingane.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1816 CE – 1887 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Zulu Kingdom</span><span class="path-step-why">Shaka turned a small chiefdom into the strongest state in southeastern Africa from 1816, and in 1879 the Zulu routed a British force at Isandlwana.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Zulu Kingdom was a powerful state in south-eastern Africa built by Shaka from the 1810s onward through military reform and conquest.</span></span></a></li></ol>
